[{"category":"section","location":"assets/fonts/Sunflower-LICENSE/#Sunflower-Font-License","page":"Sunflower Font License","text":"MadeType\nbehance.com/madetype\n\nhttps://www.fontspring.com/fonts/madetype\nhttps://creativemarket.com/MadeType\nhttps://www.youworkforthem.com/designer/1002/madetype/\nhttps://crella.net/store/madetype/\nhttps://thehungryjpeg.com/madetype\n\n\nEND USER LICENSE AGREEMENT\n\n- FOR PERSONAL USE. \n\n- Contact me at Madetypeinfo@gmail.com before commercial using it.\n\n- MadeType is not liable for any damage resulting from the use ot this typeface.\n\n- All rights are retained by MadeType.\n\n\nTHANK YOU!","title":"Sunflower Font License"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/1-intro/#ToQUBO.jl-Booklet","page":"Introduction","text":"This booklet aims to gather the theoretical and practical details behind ToQUBO and provide documentation for project internals. The target audience includes, among others, advanced users and those willing to contribute to the project. The latter are advised to read the following sections, as they give a glimpse of the ideas employed up to now.","title":"ToQUBO.jl Booklet"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/1-intro/#Table-of-Contents","page":"Introduction","text":"Pages = [\"2-qubo.md\", \"3-pbo.md\", \"4-encoding.md\", \"5-virtual.md\", \"6-compiler.md\", \"7-solvers.md\", \"8-appendix.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Table of Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Gathering-Results","page":"Gathering Results","text":"After running optimize!(model), you can retrieve various information about the solutions and the solving process.","title":"Gathering Results"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Solution-Status","page":"Gathering Results","text":"","title":"Solution Status"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Termination-Status","page":"Gathering Results","text":"The termination status indicates how the optimization process ended:\n\nstatus = termination_status(model)\n\nCommon termination statuses include:\n\nOPTIMAL: The solver found an optimal solution\nLOCALLY_SOLVED: A locally optimal solution was found\nTIME_LIMIT: The solver stopped due to time limit\nITERATION_LIMIT: The solver stopped due to iteration limit","title":"Termination Status"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Primal-Status","page":"Gathering Results","text":"The primal status indicates the quality of the primal solution:\n\npstatus = primal_status(model)\n\nA status of FEASIBLE_POINT indicates that a valid solution was found.","title":"Primal Status"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Solution-Summary","page":"Gathering Results","text":"For a quick overview of the optimization result:\n\nsolution_summary(model)","title":"Solution Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Retrieving-Variable-Values","page":"Gathering Results","text":"","title":"Retrieving Variable Values"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Single-Solution","page":"Gathering Results","text":"Get the value of a single variable:\n\nx_value = value(x)\n\nGet values of an array of variables:\n\nx_values = value.(x)","title":"Single Solution"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Multiple-Solutions","page":"Gathering Results","text":"Many QUBO solvers, especially quantum annealers and samplers, return multiple solutions. Access them using the result keyword:\n\n# Number of solutions found\nn_solutions = result_count(model)\n\n# Access the i-th solution\nfor i in 1:n_solutions\n    xi = value.(x, result = i)\n    println(\"Solution $i: $xi\")\nend\n\ntip: Best Solution\nBy default (without specifying result), value returns the best solution found.","title":"Multiple Solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Feasibility-and-Constraint-Violations","page":"Gathering Results","text":"For constrained models, inspect feasibility separately from the objective value. violations reports the source constraints violated by a sampled result after projecting the QUBO solution back to the source variables. is_feasible checks one result, and feasibility_report summarizes one or more results.\n\nviolated = ToQUBO.violations(model; result = 1, atol = 1e-6)\nok = ToQUBO.is_feasible(model; result = 1)\nreport = ToQUBO.feasibility_report(model)\n\nFor JuMP models, pass the JuMP.Model directly. For low-level MOI usage, pass the ToQUBO.Optimizer.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing QUBODrivers\nusing ToQUBO\nusing ToQUBO: Attributes\n\nmodel = Model(() -> ToQUBO.Optimizer(ExactSampler.Optimizer))\n@variable(model, x[1:2], Bin)\n@objective(model, Max, 3x[1] + 3x[2])\ncapacity = @constraint(model, x[1] + x[2] <= 1)\n\n# Deliberately undersize this penalty to demonstrate infeasibility reporting.\nset_attribute(capacity, Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint(), -0.1)\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nviolated = ToQUBO.violations(model; result = 1)\nfirst(violated).violation\n\nThe feasibility helpers use the same result indexing as value and objective_value, so sampling runs can be summarized as rates:\n\nreport = ToQUBO.feasibility_report(model; result = 1:result_count(model))\nreport.feasible_count / report.result_count\n\nConstraint violations are measured as follows:\n\nSource constraint type Violation measure\nVariable bounds and scalar affine/quadratic EqualTo, LessThan, GreaterThan, Interval MOI.Utilities.distance_to_set(value, set)\nVectorOfVariables in SOS1 Sum of absolute values outside the largest-magnitude entry\nAffine/quadratic Indicator Zero when inactive; otherwise the inner scalar-set violation\n\nEach ConstraintViolation includes the source constraint index, function type, set type, evaluated source-function value, raw residual, nonnegative violation magnitude, and projected values for variables referenced by that constraint. For encoded integer variables, the projected source values are the compiler's decoded values for the sampled binary state; continuous discretizations report the decoded grid value.","title":"Feasibility and Constraint Violations"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Objective-Value","page":"Gathering Results","text":"","title":"Objective Value"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Single-Solution-2","page":"Gathering Results","text":"Get the objective value of the best solution:\n\nobj = objective_value(model)","title":"Single Solution"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Multiple-Solutions-2","page":"Gathering Results","text":"Get the objective value for a specific solution:\n\nobj_i = objective_value(model, result = i)","title":"Multiple Solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Compilation-Information","page":"Gathering Results","text":"ToQUBO.jl provides attributes to inspect the compilation process:\n\nusing MathOptInterface as MOI\nusing ToQUBO: Attributes","title":"Compilation Information"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Compilation-Time","page":"Gathering Results","text":"Get the time spent converting your model to QUBO form:\n\ncomp_time = MOI.get(model, Attributes.CompilationTime())","title":"Compilation Time"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Compilation-Status","page":"Gathering Results","text":"Check the status of the compilation:\n\ncomp_status = MOI.get(model, Attributes.CompilationStatus())","title":"Compilation Status"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Source-and-Target-Models","page":"Gathering Results","text":"Access the intermediate models created during compilation:\n\n# Original model (with MOI bridges applied)\nsource = MOI.get(model, Attributes.SourceModel())\n\n# QUBO formulation\ntarget = MOI.get(model, Attributes.TargetModel())","title":"Source and Target Models"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Formulation-Introspection","page":"Gathering Results","text":"Advanced users can inspect the pseudo-boolean functions generated by the compiler:\n\n# Parsed source objective, before penalties are added\nf = MOI.get(model, Attributes.CompiledObjectiveFunction())\n\n# Final compiled Hamiltonian, after penalties and quadratization\nH = MOI.get(model, Attributes.CompiledHamiltonian())\n\n# Target QUBO variables and expansion for a source variable\ny = MOI.get(model, Attributes.VariableTargetVariables(), variable_ref)\nxi = MOI.get(model, Attributes.VariableEncodingFunction(), variable_ref)\n\n# Penalty function for a source constraint, before multiplying by rho\ng = MOI.get(model, Attributes.ConstraintEncodingFunction(), constraint_ref)\n\n# Slack-variable information generated for an inequality constraint, if any\nz = MOI.get(model, Attributes.SlackVariableTargetVariables(), constraint_ref)\nzeta = MOI.get(model, Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingFunction(), constraint_ref)","title":"Formulation Introspection"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Reformulation-Metadata","page":"Gathering Results","text":"ToQUBO records JSON-compatible reformulation metadata for downstream tools that need to interpret full QUBO states. The metadata uses string-keyed dictionaries, arrays, primitive values, and finite numeric values. It is available directly from the optimizer. QUBOTools.backend does not attach the full metadata payload by default, so public QUBO extraction can avoid materializing large JSON-compatible term dictionaries. Opt in with full_metadata = true when the returned QUBOTools model needs embedded metadata under metadata[\"toqubo\"][\"reformulation\"].\n\nusing ToQUBO: QUBOTools\n\nmetadata = ToQUBO.reformulation_metadata(JuMP.unsafe_backend(model))\n\nqubo_model = QUBOTools.backend(model; full_metadata = true)\nmetadata = ToQUBO.reformulation_metadata(qubo_model)\n\noriginal = ToQUBO.original_variables(metadata)\nauxiliary = ToQUBO.auxiliary_variables(metadata)\n\nqubo_state = [0, 1, 0, 1]\nsource_state = ToQUBO.project_original_state(metadata, qubo_state)\n\nThe metadata guarantees the original source variable order, target QUBO variable order, source-to-target expansion_variables and expansion_terms, constraint/slack ownership for generated target variables, penalty terms recorded by the compiler, and an applied_penalties section with the applied constraint, variable-encoding, and slack-variable penalty coefficients. applied_penalties is a convenience view of values also stored on the corresponding metadata records. This is enough to project a full QUBO state back to original variables. Exact auxiliary consistency checks and repair remain encoding-specific and are not guaranteed by this metadata contract.\n\nFull reformulation metadata is intentionally generated on request. Dense models can create one metadata record per source-to-target expansion term and per constraint-encoding term, so the JSON-compatible payload may allocate gigabytes on large TSP-style formulations. Use the default QUBOTools.backend(model) path when you only need the compiled QUBO, and use QUBOTools.backend(model; full_metadata = true) or ToQUBO.reformulation_metadata(JuMP.unsafe_backend(model)) when you need projection and interpretation metadata.","title":"Reformulation Metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Penalty-Information","page":"Gathering Results","text":"Constraints are converted to penalty terms in QUBO. You can retrieve the penalty coefficients used:","title":"Penalty Information"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Constraint-Penalties","page":"Gathering Results","text":"# Get the penalty used for a specific constraint\nrho = MOI.get(model, Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenalty(), constraint_ref)\n\n# Equivalent reporting-oriented alias\nrho = MOI.get(model, Attributes.AppliedPenalty(), constraint_ref)","title":"Constraint Penalties"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Variable-Encoding-Penalties","page":"Gathering Results","text":"For encoded variables:\n\n# Get the penalty used for variable encoding\ntheta = MOI.get(model, Attributes.VariableEncodingPenalty(), variable_ref)","title":"Variable Encoding Penalties"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Example:-Complete-Results-Retrieval","page":"Gathering Results","text":"using JuMP\nusing ToQUBO\nusing QUBODrivers  # Provides ExactSampler and other solvers\n\n# Create and solve a model\nmodel = Model(() -> ToQUBO.Optimizer(ExactSampler.Optimizer))\n\n@variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n@constraint(model, c1, x[1] + x[2] <= 1)\n@objective(model, Max, x[1] + 2*x[2] + 3*x[3])\n\noptimize!(model)\n\n# Check status\nprintln(\"Termination: \", termination_status(model))\nprintln(\"Primal: \", primal_status(model))\n\n# Get best solution\nif primal_status(model) == FEASIBLE_POINT\n    println(\"Best solution: \", value.(x))\n    println(\"Best objective: \", objective_value(model))\nend\n\n# Iterate over all solutions\nprintln(\"\\nAll solutions:\")\nfor i in 1:result_count(model)\n    println(\"  Solution $i: x = $(value.(x, result = i)), obj = $(objective_value(model, result = i))\")\nend","title":"Example: Complete Results Retrieval"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Working-with-QUBOTools","page":"Gathering Results","text":"ToQUBO.jl integrates with QUBOTools.jl for advanced QUBO manipulation. You can extract the compiled QUBO from a JuMP model after optimize!:\n\nusing JuMP\nusing ToQUBO\nusing ToQUBO: QUBOTools\n\nmodel = Model(ToQUBO.Optimizer)\n@variable(model, x[1:2], Bin)\n@constraint(model, x[1] + x[2] <= 1)\n@objective(model, Min, x[1] - 2x[2])\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nqubo_model = QUBOTools.backend(model)\nn, linear, quadratic, scale, offset = ToQUBO.qubo(model, :dense)\n\nThis allows you to:\n\nExport the QUBO to various file formats\nAnalyze the QUBO structure\nUse additional QUBOTools functionality","title":"Working with QUBOTools"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-results/#Next-Steps","page":"Gathering Results","text":"Learn about Compiler Settings to customize the reformulation\nExplore Examples in QUBO.jl for end-to-end application guides","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.reformulation_metadata","page":"Gathering Results","text":"reformulation_metadata(model_or_metadata)\n\nReturn JSON-compatible ToQUBO reformulation metadata. For a live ToQUBO.Optimizer, the metadata is generated from the compiled virtual model. For a QUBOTools.AbstractModel or a metadata dictionary, the metadata is read from metadata[\"toqubo\"][\"reformulation\"]. QUBOTools.backend skips this full metadata payload by default; call QUBOTools.backend(model; full_metadata = true) when a returned QUBOTools model needs embedded reformulation metadata.\n\nThe applied_penalties section is a convenience view. Its entries duplicate the applied penalty coefficients also attached to the corresponding variable, constraint, and slack-variable metadata records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.reformulation_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.original_variables","page":"Gathering Results","text":"original_variables(model_or_metadata)\n\nReturn the original source variable identities in compiler order.\n\nFor a live ToQUBO.Optimizer, the result is a vector of MOI.VariableIndex values. For serialized metadata or a QUBOTools.AbstractModel, the result is a vector of integer variable ids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.original_variables"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.auxiliary_variables","page":"Gathering Results","text":"auxiliary_variables(model_or_metadata)\n\nReturn target QUBO variables that are not owned by original source variables.\n\nFor a live ToQUBO.Optimizer, the result is a vector of MOI.VariableIndex values. For serialized metadata or a QUBOTools.AbstractModel, the result is a vector of integer target variable ids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.auxiliary_variables"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.project_original_state","page":"Gathering Results","text":"project_original_state(model_or_metadata, qubo_state)\n\nProject a full QUBO state onto the original source variables using the stored encoding expansions.\n\nqubo_state may be a vector indexed by target QUBO variable id, or a dictionary keyed by integer target ids, string target ids, or MOI.VariableIndex values. For a live ToQUBO.Optimizer, the result is keyed by MOI.VariableIndex. For serialized metadata or a QUBOTools.AbstractModel, the result is keyed by integer source variable ids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.project_original_state"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.qubo","page":"Gathering Results","text":"qubo(model, args...; kwargs...)\n\nReturn the compiled QUBO representation for a model supported by QUBOTools.\n\nThis is a thin public ToQUBO re-export of QUBOTools.qubo. For a JuMP model using ToQUBO.Optimizer, call optimize! first, then pass the JuMP.Model directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.qubo"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.ConstraintViolation","page":"Gathering Results","text":"ConstraintViolation\n\nA measured source-constraint residual for one sampled result.\n\nconstraint is the source MOI.ConstraintIndex, function_type and set_type identify the constraint family, value is the original constraint function evaluated on the projected source-variable values, raw_residual is the set-specific residual before applying tolerances, violation is the nonnegative distance to the set, and variable_values contains the projected values for variables referenced by the constraint function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.ConstraintViolation"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.FeasibilityResult","page":"Gathering Results","text":"FeasibilityResult\n\nSummary of source-constraint feasibility for one sampled result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.FeasibilityResult"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.FeasibilityReport","page":"Gathering Results","text":"FeasibilityReport\n\nPer-result source-constraint feasibility summary returned by feasibility_report.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.FeasibilityReport"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.violations","page":"Gathering Results","text":"violations(model; result::Int = 1, atol::Real = 1e-6)\n\nReturn source constraints violated by sampled result result.\n\nThe sampled QUBO result is projected back to the original source variables using the compiler's virtual mapping, then each source constraint is evaluated and measured against its MOI set. Scalar sets use MOI.Utilities.distance_to_set. Indicator constraints are considered violated only when their activation value triggers the inner set; SOS1 constraints use the distance to the nearest one-nonzero vector under the ell_1 projection convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.violations"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.is_feasible","page":"Gathering Results","text":"is_feasible(model; result::Int = 1, atol::Real = 1e-6)\n\nReturn true if every source constraint is satisfied by sampled result result within absolute tolerance atol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.is_feasible"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.feasibility_report","page":"Gathering Results","text":"feasibility_report(model; result = nothing, atol::Real = 1e-6)\n\nReturn a FeasibilityReport summarizing source-constraint feasibility.\n\nIf result is nothing, all available solver results are summarized. Pass an integer or iterable of result indices to summarize selected samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.feasibility_report"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.CompilationTime","page":"Gathering Results","text":"CompilationTime()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.CompilationTime"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.CompilationStatus","page":"Gathering Results","text":"CompilationStatus()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.CompilationStatus"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.SourceModel","page":"Gathering Results","text":"SourceModel()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.SourceModel"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.TargetModel","page":"Gathering Results","text":"TargetModel()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.TargetModel"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.CompiledObjectiveFunction","page":"Gathering Results","text":"CompiledObjectiveFunction()\n\nReturn the pseudo-boolean objective parsed from the source model, before penalty terms are added.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.CompiledObjectiveFunction"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.CompiledHamiltonian","page":"Gathering Results","text":"CompiledHamiltonian()\n\nReturn the final pseudo-boolean function written to the target QUBO model, after penalties and quadratization are applied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.CompiledHamiltonian"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableTargetVariables","page":"Gathering Results","text":"VariableTargetVariables()\n\nReturn the target QUBO variables generated for a source variable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableTargetVariables"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingFunction","page":"Gathering Results","text":"VariableEncodingFunction()\n\nReturn the pseudo-boolean expansion used to represent a source variable in terms of target QUBO variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingFunction"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingPenaltyFunction","page":"Gathering Results","text":"VariableEncodingPenaltyFunction()\n\nReturn the pseudo-boolean penalty function generated by a variable encoding, if that encoding requires one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingPenaltyFunction"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingFunction","page":"Gathering Results","text":"ConstraintEncodingFunction()\n\nReturn the pseudo-boolean penalty function generated for a source constraint, before multiplying by its penalty coefficient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingFunction"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.AppliedPenalty","page":"Gathering Results","text":"AppliedPenalty()\n\nReturn the applied source-constraint penalty coefficient after compilation.\n\nThis is an alias for ConstraintEncodingPenalty intended for reporting code that wants a generic \"applied penalty\" query.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.AppliedPenalty"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableTargetVariables","page":"Gathering Results","text":"SlackVariableTargetVariables()\n\nReturn the target QUBO variables generated for a constraint slack variable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableTargetVariables"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingFunction","page":"Gathering Results","text":"SlackVariableEncodingFunction()\n\nReturn the pseudo-boolean expansion used to represent a constraint slack variable in terms of target QUBO variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingFunction"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-results/#ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingPenaltyFunction","page":"Gathering Results","text":"SlackVariableEncodingPenaltyFunction()\n\nReturn the pseudo-boolean penalty function generated by a slack-variable encoding, if that encoding requires one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingPenaltyFunction"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#Encoding-Methods","page":"Encoding","text":"","title":"Encoding Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#Variables","page":"Encoding","text":"As you may already know, QUBO models are comprised only of binary variables. So when we are reformulating general optimization problems, one important step is to encode variables into binary ones.\n\nToQUBO currently implements 6 base encoding techniques. Each method introduces a different number of variables, quadratic terms and linear terms. Also, they differ in the magnitude of their coefficients Delta.\n\nEncoding Binary Variables # Linear terms # Quadratic terms Delta\nBinary O(log n) O(log n) - O(n)\nUnary O(n) O(n) - O(1)\nOne-Hot O(n) O(n) O(n^2) O(n)\nDomain-Wall O(n) O(n) O(n) O(n)\nBounded-Coefficient O(n) O(n) - O(1)\nArithmetic Prog O(sqrtn) O(sqrtn) - O(sqrtn)","title":"Variables"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#Mirror-Encoding","page":"Encoding","text":"","title":"Mirror Encoding"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#Interval-Encoding","page":"Encoding","text":"","title":"Interval Encoding"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#Bounded-Coefficients","page":"Encoding","text":"","title":"Bounded Coefficients"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#Arbitrary-Set-Encoding","page":"Encoding","text":"","title":"Arbitrary Set Encoding"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#Semi-Domain-Encoding","page":"Encoding","text":"","title":"Semi-Domain Encoding"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#Representation-Error","page":"Encoding","text":"Let setx_i_i in k be the collection of k evenly spaced samples from the discretization of an interval a b subseteq mathbbR.\n\nThe representation error for a given point x with respect to setx_i_i in k is\n\ne_k(x) = min_i in k leftx - x_iright\n\nAssuming that x behaves as a uniformly distributed random variable, the expected absolute encoding error is\n\nbeginalign*\nmathbbElefte_k(x)right = frac1b - a int_a^b e_k(x) mathrmdx \n                              = frac14 fracb - ak - 1\nendalign*\n\nThus, for encoding methods that rely on the regular division of an interval, it is possible to define the number of samples k necessary to limit the expected error according to an upper bound tau, that is,\n\nmathbbElefte_k(x)right le tau implies k ge 1 + fracb - a4 tau\n\nThis allows the compiler to automatically infer the number of bits to allocate for an encoded variable given the tolerance factor.","title":"Representation Error"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#Constraints","page":"Encoding","text":"A QUBO model is unconstrained. So when ToQUBO is reformulating a problem, it needs to encode all constraints into the objective function losing as little information as possible.\n\nAs constraints are introduced into the objective function, we need to make sure that they won't be violated. In order to do that, ToQUBO multiplies the encoded constraint by a large penalty rho, so that any violation would result in a sub-optimal solution to the problem.\n\nEquality constraints use a squared residual penalty by default. If the parsed residual is provably nonnegative, the compiler can add the residual directly instead of squaring it, preserving the same minimizer at zero while avoiding unnecessary higher-order terms. The usual automatic penalty inference still applies to this sign-definite shortcut. The compiler can also encode an equality constraint with a signed linear residual through ToQUBO.Attributes.LinearPenalty. This option follows the linear Ising penalty method studied by Mirkarimi et al.[Mirkarimi2024PRR] and demonstrated experimentally for quantum annealing.[Mirkarimi2024NJP] It is a heuristic method: the sign and magnitude of rho affect whether the residual discourages the intended violations, so ToQUBO requires an explicit ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint instead of inferring rho automatically.\n\nSometimes, the encoding process might introduce higher-order terms, demanding ToQUBO to reduce the offending polynomials back to a quadratic form.\n\n[Mirkarimi2024PRR]: Puya Mirkarimi, Ishaan Shukla, David C. Hoyle, Ross Williams, and Nicholas Chancellor. Quantum optimization with linear Ising penalty functions for customer data science. Physical Review Research 6, 043241 (2024). {doi}\n\n[Mirkarimi2024NJP]: Puya Mirkarimi, David C. Hoyle, Ross Williams, and Nicholas Chancellor. Experimental demonstration of improved quantum optimization with linear Ising penalties. New Journal of Physics 26, 103005 (2024). {doi}","title":"Constraints"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.encode","page":"Encoding","text":"encode(var, e::VariableEncodingMethod, x::Union{VI,Nothing}, S)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.encode"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.encode!","page":"Encoding","text":"encode!(target, source...)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.encode!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.encodes","page":"Encoding","text":"encodes(f::AbstractPBF, S::Tuple{T,T}, tol::T) where {T}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.encodes"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.VariableEncodingMethod","page":"Encoding","text":"VariableEncodingMethod\n\nAbstract type for variable encoding methods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.VariableEncodingMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.Mirror","page":"Encoding","text":"Mirror()\n\nSimply mirrors a binary variable x in mathbbB with a twin variable y in mathbbB.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.Mirror"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.IntervalVariableEncodingMethod","page":"Encoding","text":"IntervalVariableEncodingMethod\n\nAbstract type for methods that encode variables using a linear function, e.g.,\n\nxi(mathbfy) = beta + sum_i = 1^n gamma_i y_i\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.IntervalVariableEncodingMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.Unary","page":"Encoding","text":"Unary{T}()\n\nInteger\n\nLet x in a b subset mathbbZ, n = b - a and mathbfy in mathbbB^n.\n\nxia b(mathbfy) = a + sum_j = 1^b - a y_j\n\nReal\n\nGiven n in mathbbN for x in a b subset mathbbR,\n\nxia b(mathbfy) = a + fracb - an sum_j = 1^n y_j\n\nRepresentation error\n\nGiven tau  0, for the expected encoding error to be less than or equal to tau, at least\n\nn ge 1 + fracb - a4 tau\n\nbinary variables become necessary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.Unary"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.Binary","page":"Encoding","text":"Binary{T}()\n\nInteger\n\nLet x in a b subset mathbbZ, n = leftlceil log_2(b - a) + 1 rightrceil and mathbfy in mathbbB^n.\n\nxia b(mathbfy) = a + left(b - a - 2^n - 1 + 1right) y_n + sum_j = 1^n - 1 2^j - 1 y_j\n\nReal\n\nGiven n in mathbbN for x in a b subset mathbbR,\n\nxia b(mathbfy) = a + fracb - a2^n - 1 sum_j = 1^n 2^j - 1 y_j\n\nRepresentation error\n\nGiven tau  0, for the expected encoding error to be less than or equal to tau, at least\n\nn ge log_2 left1 + fracb - a4 tauright\n\nbinary variables become necessary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.Binary"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.Arithmetic","page":"Encoding","text":"Arithmetic{T}()\n\nInteger\n\nLet x in a b subset mathbbZ, n = leftlceil frac12 sqrt1 + 8 (b - a) - frac12 rightrceil and mathbfy in mathbbB^n.\n\nxia b(mathbfy) = a + left( b - a - fracn (n - 1)2 right) y_n + sum_j = 1^n - 1 j y_j\n\nReal\n\nGiven n in mathbbN for x in a b subset mathbbR,\n\nxia b(mathbfy) = a + fracb - an (n + 1) sum_j = 1^n j y_j\n\nRepresentation error\n\nGiven tau  0, for the expected encoding error to be less than or equal to tau, at least\n\nn ge frac12 left 1 + sqrt3 + frac(b - a)2 tau) right\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.Arithmetic"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.Bounded","page":"Encoding","text":"Bounded{E,T}(μ::T) where {E<:Encoding,T}\n\nThe bounded-coefficient encoding method[Karimi2019] consists in limiting the magnitude of the coefficients in the encoding expansion to a parameter mu. This can be applied to the Unary, Binary and Arithmetic encoding methods.\n\nLet xia b  mathbbB^n to a b be an encoding function over the closed interval a b. The bounded-coefficient encoding function given mu is defined as\n\nxi_mua b = xi0 delta(y_1 dots y_k) + sum_j = k + 1^r mu yj\n\n[Karimi2019]: Karimi, S. & Ronagh, P. Practical integer-to-binary mapping for quantum annealers. Quantum Inf Process 18, 94 (2019). {doi}\n\n```\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.Bounded"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.SetVariableEncodingMethod","page":"Encoding","text":"SetVariableEncodingMethod\n\nAbstract type for methods that encode variables over an arbitrary set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.SetVariableEncodingMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.OneHot","page":"Encoding","text":"OneHot{T}()\n\nThe one-hot encoding is a linear technique used to represent a variable x in setgamma_j_j in n.\n\nThe associated encoding function is combined with a constraint assuring that only one and exactly one of the expansion's variables y_j is activated at a time.\n\nxisetgamma_j_j in n(mathbfy) = sum_j = 1^n gamma_j y_j textrmst sum_j = 1^n y_j = 1\n\nWhen a variable is encoded following this approach, a penalty term of the form\n\nrho left sum_j = 1^n y_j - 1 right^2\n\nis added to the objective function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.OneHot"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.DomainWall","page":"Encoding","text":"DomainWall{T}()\n\nThe Domain Wall[Chancellor2019] encoding method is a sequential approach that requires n - 1 bits to represent n distinct values.\n\nxisetgamma_j_j in n(mathbfy) = sum_j = 1^n gamma_j (y_j - y_j + 1) textrmst sum_j = 1^n y_j oplus y_j + 1 = 1 y_1 = 1 y_n + 1 = 0\n\nwhere mathbfy in mathbbB^n + 1.\n\n[Chancellor2019]: Nicholas Chancellor, Domain wall encoding of discrete variables for quantum annealing and QAOA, Quantum Science Technology 4, 2019.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.DomainWall"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.Semi","page":"Encoding","text":"Semi(e::VariableEncodingMethod)\n\nWraps another variable encoding method to represent x in 0 cup X. If the wrapped method encodes X as xiX(y), then Semi uses an additional activation bit z and encodes the semi-domain as z xiX(y).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.Semi"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.encoding_bits","page":"Encoding","text":"encoding_bits(e::VariableEncodingMethod, S::Tuple{T,T}, tol::T) where {T}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.encoding_bits"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/4-encoding/#ToQUBO.Encoding.encoding_points","page":"Encoding","text":"encoding_points(e::SetVariableEncodingMethod, S::Tuple{T,T}, tol::T) where {T}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Encoding.encoding_points"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-settings/#Compiler-Settings","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"","title":"Compiler Settings"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-settings/#Compiler-Messages","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"","title":"Compiler Messages"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-settings/#Constraint-Feasibility-Actions","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"","title":"Constraint Feasibility Actions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-settings/#Compiler-Optimization","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"","title":"Compiler Optimization"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-settings/#Working-with-target-architectures","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"","title":"Working with target architectures"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-settings/#Quadratization","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"","title":"Quadratization"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-settings/#Variable-and-Constraint-Encoding","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"","title":"Variable & Constraint Encoding"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-settings/#Automatic-Penalty-Inference","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"When a constraint, variable encoding, or slack-variable encoding needs a penalty coefficient and no explicit hint is set, ToQUBO infers one automatically. The default ToQUBO.Attributes.ObjectiveRangePenalty policy uses an objective-range exact-penalty bound:\n\nrho = s cdot sigma cdot left(fracU - L + betaepsilonright)\n\nwhere s is ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyScale, β is ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyOffset, σ is 1 for minimization models and -1 for maximization models, U - L is the compiled pseudo-boolean objective range, and ϵ is the smallest positive value of the generated nonnegative penalty function. With the default scale s = 1 and β > 0, any infeasible assignment is penalized by more than the largest possible objective improvement available within the compiled objective range. With custom scales, the same sufficient exactness condition is s * (U - L + β) > U - L; smaller scales can make the penalty a tuning heuristic rather than a certified exact penalty.\n\nThe objective range is computed from interval bounds on the compiled PBF over encoded target binary variables. For normal finite JuMP/MOI models this bound is finite after encoding, but it may overestimate the exact source-objective span when an encoding has redundant target states or feasibility relations such as one-hot sums. That overestimate keeps the penalty sufficient, but it is not always the tightest possible objective range.\n\nWhen the objective range or positive penalty gap cannot be certified, ToQUBO falls back for that coefficient to ToQUBO.Attributes.LegacyPenalty:\n\nrho = s cdot sigma cdot left(fracdeltaepsilon + betaright)\n\nwhere δ is the historical objective gap estimate from PBO.maxgap. The selected policy, objective bounds, inferred penalties with their ϵ sources, and any fallback reasons are exposed through ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyPolicyMetadata and the \"penalty_policy\" field in reformulation metadata.\n\nThe precedence is:\n\nExplicit hints such as ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint are used as-is.\nConstraint overrides ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintPenaltyScale and ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintPenaltyOffset apply to the source constraint and to the slack-variable encoding penalty generated for that constraint.\nGlobal ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyPolicy, ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyScale, and ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyOffset apply everywhere else.\n\nThe defaults are PenaltyPolicy() == ObjectiveRangePenalty(), PenaltyScale() == 1.0, and PenaltyOffset() == 1.0. The coefficient is positive for minimization models and negative for maximization models, so explicit hints should use the same sign as the coefficient you want the compiler to apply.\n\nUse the controls according to how much of the model you want to change:\n\nUse ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyPolicy to select the automatic inference policy. Set it to ToQUBO.Attributes.LegacyPenalty only when you need to reproduce the historical maxgap-based coefficients.\nUse ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyScale and ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyOffset to change all automatically inferred penalties while preserving their policy-dependent objective and penalty-gap dependence.\nUse ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintPenaltyScale and ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintPenaltyOffset when one source constraint needs a different automatic penalty. These overrides also apply to the slack-variable encoding penalty generated for that constraint.\nUse explicit hints such as ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint, ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingPenaltyHint, or ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingPenaltyHint when you need a fixed coefficient. Hints bypass the automatic scale and offset settings.\n\nnote: ToQUBO-specific settings\nThese attributes control the ToQUBO compiler, so the reference for setting, overriding, and retrieving penalty coefficients belongs in this manual. End-to-end ecosystem examples can live in the QUBO.jl documentation and link back here when they tune ToQUBO penalties.","title":"Automatic Penalty Inference"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-settings/#Changing-Penalty-Values","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"For feasibility tuning, start by inspecting ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyPolicyMetadata and sweeping PenaltyScale() while keeping explicit hints unset. If only one constraint needs adjustment, override that constraint's automatic scale or offset. Pin exact penalty values only when you have a known coefficient to apply.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing QUBODrivers\nusing ToQUBO\nusing ToQUBO: Attributes, Encoding\n\n# Use an exact sampler for a small example. Replace it with your QUBO solver.\nmodel = Model(() -> ToQUBO.Optimizer(ExactSampler.Optimizer))\n@variable(model, x[1:2], Bin)\n@variable(model, 0 <= z <= 3, Int)\n@objective(model, Min, 3x[1] + x[2] + z)\n\ncapacity = @constraint(model, x[1] + x[2] <= 1)\nassignment = @constraint(model, x[1] + x[2] == 1)\n\n# Model-wide automatic penalty tuning.\nset_attribute(model, Attributes.PenaltyScale(), 2.0)\nset_attribute(model, Attributes.PenaltyOffset(), 1.0)\n\n# Automatic tuning for one source constraint and its slack encoding.\nset_attribute(capacity, Attributes.ConstraintPenaltyScale(), 4.0)\nset_attribute(capacity, Attributes.ConstraintPenaltyOffset(), 2.0)\n\n# Fixed values for specific generated penalties.\nset_attribute(assignment, Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint(), 12.0)\nset_attribute(capacity, Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingMethod(), Encoding.OneHot())\nset_attribute(capacity, Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingPenaltyHint(), 9.0)\nset_attribute(z, Attributes.VariableEncodingMethod(), Encoding.OneHot())\nset_attribute(z, Attributes.VariableEncodingPenaltyHint(), 6.0)\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nrho_capacity = get_attribute(capacity, Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenalty())\nrho_assignment = get_attribute(assignment, Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenalty())\neta_capacity = get_attribute(capacity, Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingPenalty())\ntheta_z = get_attribute(z, Attributes.VariableEncodingPenalty())\npolicy_metadata = get_attribute(model, Attributes.PenaltyPolicyMetadata())\n\n# The capacity penalty is inferred by the automatic policy.\n@assert rho_capacity !== nothing\n@assert rho_assignment == 12.0\n@assert eta_capacity == 9.0\n@assert theta_z == 6.0\n@assert policy_metadata[\"policy\"] == \"ObjectiveRangePenalty\"\n\nVery small ϵ values or broad objective ranges can produce large penalties. Inspect the policy metadata before overriding the policy or pinning explicit penalties.","title":"Changing Penalty Values"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-settings/#Constraint-Penalty-Methods","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"Equality constraints are encoded with ToQUBO.Attributes.QuadraticPenalty by default. This normally squares the residual and lets the compiler infer a penalty coefficient when ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint is not set. When the parsed residual is provably nonnegative, the compiler can instead add the residual directly to avoid unnecessary higher-order terms while preserving the same minimizer at zero. The same automatic penalty inference applies to this sign-definite shortcut.\n\nUse ToQUBO.Attributes.LinearPenalty only when you want an equality constraint encoded as its signed residual. Since this form can make infeasible assignments cheaper when the sign or magnitude is wrong, automatic penalty inference is disabled. Every equality constraint that uses LinearPenalty() must also have an explicit ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint.\n\nThe method is motivated by the linear Ising penalty approach of Mirkarimi et al.[Mirkarimi2024PRR], its quantum annealing demonstration,[Mirkarimi2024NJP] and the accompanying Durham data and code archive.[Mirkarimi2024Data] Those papers highlight the main tradeoff reflected in this API: linear penalties can avoid the extra couplings introduced by quadratic penalties, but they are heuristic and are not guaranteed to exactly enforce every constraint.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing ToQUBO\nusing ToQUBO: Attributes\n\nmodel = Model(ToQUBO.Optimizer)\n@variable(model, x[1:2], Bin)\n@objective(model, Min, 0)\nc = @constraint(model, x[1] + x[2] == 1)\n\nset_attribute(model, Attributes.DefaultConstraintEncodingMethod(), Attributes.LinearPenalty())\nset_attribute(c, Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint(), -2.0)\n\nFor mixed models, keep the default method as ToQUBO.Attributes.QuadraticPenalty and set ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingMethod only on the constraints that should use the linear residual.\n\n[Mirkarimi2024PRR]: Puya Mirkarimi, Ishaan Shukla, David C. Hoyle, Ross Williams, and Nicholas Chancellor. Quantum optimization with linear Ising penalty functions for customer data science. Physical Review Research 6, 043241 (2024). {doi}\n\n[Mirkarimi2024NJP]: Puya Mirkarimi, David C. Hoyle, Ross Williams, and Nicholas Chancellor. Experimental demonstration of improved quantum optimization with linear Ising penalties. New Journal of Physics 26, 103005 (2024). {doi}\n\n[Mirkarimi2024Data]: Puya Mirkarimi, Ishaan Shukla, David C. Hoyle, Ross Williams, and Nicholas Chancellor. Quantum optimization with linear Ising penalty functions for customer data science [dataset]. Durham University data and code archive (2024). {doi}","title":"Constraint Penalty Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-settings/#Discretization","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"","title":"Discretization"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.StableCompilation","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"StableCompilation()\n\nWhen set, this boolean flag enables stable reformulation methods, thus yielding predictable results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.StableCompilation"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.Warnings","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"Warnings()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.Warnings"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.IgnoreFeasibleConstraints","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"IgnoreFeasibleConstraints()\n\nWhen set, constraints whose encoded residual is provably always feasible are ignored instead of being added as penalty terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.IgnoreFeasibleConstraints"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.ErrorInfeasibleConstraints","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"ErrorInfeasibleConstraints()\n\nWhen set, direct scalar constraints whose encoded residual is provably infeasible stop compilation with MOI.INFEASIBLE.\n\nThis does not make infeasible inner constraints of indicator constraints fail the whole model, because those constraints are conditional on the indicator activation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.ErrorInfeasibleConstraints"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.Optimization","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"Optimization()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.Optimization"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.Architecture","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"Architecture()\n\nSelects which solver architecture to use. Defaults to QUBOTools.GenericArchitecture.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.Architecture"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.Quadratize","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"Quadratize()\n\nBoolean flag to conditionally perform the quadratization step. Is automatically set by the compiler when high-order functions are generated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.Quadratize"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.QuadratizationMethod","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"QuadratizationMethod()\n\nDefines which quadratization method to use. Available options are defined in the PBO submodule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.QuadratizationMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.StableQuadratization","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"StableQuadratization()\n\nWhen set, this boolean flag enables stable quadratization methods, thus yielding predictable results. This is intended to be used during tests or other situations where deterministic output is desired. On the other hand, usage in production is not recommended since it requires increased memory and processing resources.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.StableQuadratization"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingBits","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"VariableEncodingBits()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingBits"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.DefaultVariableEncodingBits","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"DefaultVariableEncodingBits()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.DefaultVariableEncodingBits"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingATol","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"VariableEncodingATol()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingATol"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.DefaultVariableEncodingATol","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"DefaultVariableEncodingATol()\n\nFallback value for VariableEncodingATol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.DefaultVariableEncodingATol"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingMethod","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"VariableEncodingMethod()\n\nAvailable methods are:\n\nEncoding.Binary (default)\nEncoding.Unary\nEncoding.Arithmetic\nEncoding.OneHot\nEncoding.DomainWall\nEncoding.Bounded\n\nThe Encoding.Binary, Encoding.Unary and Encoding.Arithmetic encodings can have their expansion coefficients bounded by wrapping them with the Encoding.Bounded method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.DefaultVariableEncodingMethod","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"DefaultVariableEncodingMethod()\n\nFallback value for VariableEncodingMethod.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.DefaultVariableEncodingMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingPenaltyHint","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"VariableEncodingPenaltyHint()\n\nSet a fixed penalty coefficient for a variable encoding that generates a penalty function.\n\nWhen unset, ToQUBO infers the coefficient from PenaltyPolicy, PenaltyScale, PenaltyOffset, and the generated penalty function gap. The hint is used as-is and bypasses automatic inference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingPenaltyHint"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingPenalty","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"VariableEncodingPenalty()\n\nReturn the applied variable-encoding penalty coefficient after compilation.\n\nThis returns nothing when the variable encoding does not generate a penalty function. Use VariableEncodingPenaltyHint before compilation to pin a specific coefficient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingPenalty"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingMethod","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"SlackVariableEncodingMethod()\n\nSets the encoding method for slack variables generated by constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingATol","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"SlackVariableEncodingATol()\n\nSets the tolerance for slack variables generated by constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingATol"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingBits","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"SlackVariableEncodingBits()\n\nSets the number of bits for slack variables generated by constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingBits"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingPenaltyHint","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"SlackVariableEncodingPenaltyHint()\n\nSet a fixed penalty coefficient for a slack-variable encoding generated by a source constraint.\n\nWhen unset, ToQUBO infers the coefficient from the automatic penalty policy. ConstraintPenaltyScale and ConstraintPenaltyOffset apply to the source constraint and to its generated slack-variable encoding penalty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingPenaltyHint"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingPenalty","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"SlackVariableEncodingPenalty()\n\nReturn the applied slack-variable encoding penalty coefficient after compilation.\n\nThis returns nothing when the constraint does not generate a slack-variable encoding penalty. Use SlackVariableEncodingPenaltyHint before compilation to pin a specific coefficient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.SlackVariableEncodingPenalty"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.ObjectiveRangePenalty","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"ObjectiveRangePenalty()\n\nInfer automatic penalty coefficients from a sufficient exact-penalty bound based on the compiled objective range. When ToQUBO can certify finite objective bounds and a positive violation gap for a generated nonnegative penalty function, penalties are inferred as scale * sigma * ((objective_range + margin)  epsilon).\n\nThis is the default policy. If the bound cannot be certified for a particular penalty function, ToQUBO falls back to LegacyPenalty for that coefficient and records the fallback in reformulation metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.ObjectiveRangePenalty"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.LegacyPenalty","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"LegacyPenalty()\n\nUse the historical ToQUBO automatic penalty heuristic scale * sigma * (delta  epsilon + beta), where delta is computed with PBO.maxgap on the compiled source objective. This policy remains available for compatibility and as a fallback when ObjectiveRangePenalty cannot be certified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.LegacyPenalty"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyPolicy","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"PenaltyPolicy()\n\nSelect the automatic penalty inference policy used when no explicit penalty hint is set. The default is ObjectiveRangePenalty, which uses a certified objective-range exact-penalty bound when available and falls back to LegacyPenalty otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyPolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyPolicyMetadata","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"PenaltyPolicyMetadata()\n\nReturn diagnostic metadata for the automatic penalty policy used during the last compilation.\n\nThe returned dictionary includes the selected automatic policy, objective bounds, the legacy objective gap, fallback records, and inferred_penalties. inferred_penalties is grouped under \"constraints\", \"variables\", and \"slack_variables\". Each entry records the source item kind and id, final automatic coefficient, epsilon, epsilon_source, scale, offset, automatic policy, selected policy, and any fallback reason.\n\nOnly automatically inferred coefficients are included in inferred_penalties. Explicit penalty hints bypass automatic inference; use AppliedPenalty or reformulation metadata's applied_penalties section when a complete list of applied coefficients is needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyPolicyMetadata"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyOffset","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"PenaltyOffset()\n\nSet the global offset or margin used by automatic penalty inference.\n\nUnder ObjectiveRangePenalty, this is the objective-range margin in scale * sigma * ((objective_range + beta)  epsilon). Under LegacyPenalty, this is the historical additive offset in scale * sigma * (delta  epsilon + beta). The default is 1.0. Use ConstraintPenaltyOffset to override this value for a specific source constraint and its slack-variable encoding penalty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyOffset"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyScale","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"PenaltyScale()\n\nSet the global multiplier applied to automatically inferred penalty coefficients.\n\nWhen no explicit penalty hint is set, ToQUBO infers penalty coefficients from PenaltyPolicy. The default scale is 1.0. Use ConstraintPenaltyScale to override this value for a specific source constraint and its slack-variable encoding penalty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.PenaltyScale"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintPenaltyOffset","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"ConstraintPenaltyOffset()\n\nOverride PenaltyOffset for a specific source constraint.\n\nThe override applies to automatically inferred source-constraint penalties and to the slack-variable encoding penalty generated for the same source constraint. Explicit penalty hints still take precedence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintPenaltyOffset"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintPenaltyScale","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"ConstraintPenaltyScale()\n\nOverride PenaltyScale for a specific source constraint.\n\nThe override applies to automatically inferred source-constraint penalties and to the slack-variable encoding penalty generated for the same source constraint. Explicit penalty hints still take precedence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintPenaltyScale"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.QuadraticPenalty","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"QuadraticPenalty()\n\nEncode equality constraints with the standard squared residual penalty.\n\nIf the parsed residual is provably nonnegative, the compiler may use the residual directly instead of squaring it. This preserves the same minimizer at zero while avoiding unnecessary higher-order terms. Automatic penalty inference is still available for this sign-definite shortcut.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.QuadraticPenalty"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.LinearPenalty","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"LinearPenalty()\n\nEncode equality constraints with a signed linear residual penalty.\n\nThis method is heuristic: unlike QuadraticPenalty, it is not guaranteed to make every infeasible assignment more expensive. Use ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint to tune the signed penalty strength. Automatic penalty inference is not available for this method.\n\nThis option follows the linear Ising penalty method studied by Mirkarimi et al. in \"Quantum optimization with linear Ising penalty functions for customer data science\" (doi:10.1103/PhysRevResearch.6.043241) and \"Experimental demonstration of improved quantum optimization with linear Ising penalties\" (doi:10.1088/1367-2630/ad7e4a).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.LinearPenalty"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.DefaultConstraintEncodingMethod","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"DefaultConstraintEncodingMethod()\n\nFallback method used to reformulate equality constraints.\n\nAvailable options are:\n\nQuadraticPenalty (default)\nLinearPenalty\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.DefaultConstraintEncodingMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingMethod","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"ConstraintEncodingMethod()\n\nSets the method used to reformulate an equality constraint.\n\nWhen unset, this falls back to DefaultConstraintEncodingMethod. Inequality constraints keep the existing quadratic slack formulation. When set to LinearPenalty, the constraint must also have an explicit ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint()\n\nSet a fixed penalty coefficient for a source constraint.\n\nWhen unset, ToQUBO infers the coefficient from the automatic penalty policy, unless the constraint uses LinearPenalty. Linear penalties require an explicit hint because their sign and magnitude control whether the residual discourages infeasible assignments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenalty","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"ConstraintEncodingPenalty()\n\nReturn the applied source-constraint penalty coefficient after compilation.\n\nUse ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint before compilation to pin a specific coefficient, or ConstraintPenaltyScale and ConstraintPenaltyOffset to tune automatic inference for one source constraint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenalty"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/4-settings/#ToQUBO.Attributes.Discretize","page":"Compiler Settings","text":"Discretize()\n\nWhen set, this boolean flag guarantees that every coefficient in the final formulation is an integer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Attributes.Discretize"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/portfolio_optimization/","page":"Portfolio Optimization","text":"<!– Generated by docs/make.jl – do not edit directly. –>","title":"Portfolio Optimization"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/portfolio_optimization/#Portfolio-Optimization","page":"Portfolio Optimization","text":"This page is kept as a compatibility entry for readers following older ToQUBO.jl example links.\n\nThe canonical version now lives in the QUBO.jl documentation:\n\nPortfolio Optimization\n\nFor package-specific workflow guidance, see Running a Model. For the current ecosystem examples index, see Examples in QUBO.jl.","title":"Portfolio Optimization"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/3-pbo/#Pseudo-Boolean-Optimization","page":"PBO","text":"Internally, problems are represented through a Pseudo-Boolean Optimization (PBO) framework. The main goal is to represent a given problem using a Pseudo-Boolean Function (PBF) since there is an immediate correspondence between optimization over quadratic PBFs and the QUBO formalism.","title":"Pseudo-Boolean Optimization"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/3-pbo/#Quadratization","page":"PBO","text":"In order to successfully achieve a QUBO formulation, sometimes it is needed to quadratize the resulting PBF, i.e., reduce its degree until reaching the quadratic case. \n\nA quadratization is a mapping mathcalQ mathscrF to mathscrF^2 such that\n\nforall f in mathscrF forall x in 0 1^n min_y mathcalQleftlbracefrightrbrace(x y) = f(x)\n\nThere are many quadratization methods available[Dattani2019], and ToQUBO implements two of them for now. However, using Julia's multiple dispatch paradigm, it's possible to extend the quadratization method coverage with your own algorithms.\n\n[Dattani2019]: Nikesh S. Dattani, Quadratization in discrete optimization and quantum mechanics, ArXiv, 2019 {doi}","title":"Quadratization"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/3-pbo/#Quadratization-Methods","page":"PBO","text":"","title":"Quadratization Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/3-pbo/#Stable-Quadratization","page":"PBO","text":"The quadratization of a PBF does not guarantee that the resulting function will always be the same, as the order of terms can be different each time. This can be an issue in some situations where a deterministic output is required.\n\nWith said that, we have introduced the concept of Stable Quadratization, where the terms of the PBF are sorted, guaranteeing that the resulting PBF will be the same every time. We have defined it as an attribute of the compiler, with the  ToQUBO.Attributes.StableQuadratization flag.","title":"Stable Quadratization"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#The-Compiler","page":"The Compiler","text":"warning: Work in progress\nWe hope to write this part of the documentation soon. Please come back later!","title":"The Compiler"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#Compilation-Steps","page":"The Compiler","text":"","title":"Compilation Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#Setup","page":"The Compiler","text":"","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#Parsing","page":"The Compiler","text":"","title":"Parsing"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#Reformulation","page":"The Compiler","text":"","title":"Reformulation"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#Copying","page":"The Compiler","text":"","title":"Copying"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#Hamiltonian-Assembly","page":"The Compiler","text":"","title":"Hamiltonian Assembly"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.CompilationError","page":"The Compiler","text":"CompilationError(msg::Union{Nothing, String})\n\nThis error indicates any failure during QUBO formulation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.CompilationError"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.reset!","page":"The Compiler","text":"reset!(model::Virtual.Model, arch::AbstractArchitecture)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.reset!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.setup!","page":"The Compiler","text":"setup!(model::Virtual.Model, ::AbstractArchitecture)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.setup!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.parse!","page":"The Compiler","text":"parse!(\n    model::Virtual.Model{T},\n    g::PBO.PBF{VI,T},\n    f::MOI.AbstractFunction,\n    arch::AbstractArchitecture\n) where {T}\n\nParses the given MOI function f into PBF g.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.parse!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler._parse","page":"The Compiler","text":"\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler._parse"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.sense!","page":"The Compiler","text":"sense!(model::Virtual.Model, ::AbstractArchitecture)\n\nCopies MOI.ObjectiveSense from model.source_model to model.target_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.sense!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.variable!","page":"The Compiler","text":"variable!(model::Virtual.Model{T}) where {T}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.variable!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.variables!","page":"The Compiler","text":"variables!(model::Virtual.Model{T}) where {T}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.variables!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.constraint","page":"The Compiler","text":"constraint\n\nReturns the pseudo-boolean function associated to a given constraint from the source model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.constraint"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.constraints!","page":"The Compiler","text":"constraints!(model::Virtual.Model, ::AbstractArchitecture)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.constraints!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.objective!","page":"The Compiler","text":"objective!(model::Virtual.Model, ::AbstractArchitecture)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.objective!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.penalties!","page":"The Compiler","text":"penalties!(model::Virtual.Model, arch::AbstractArchitecture)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.penalties!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.isqubo","page":"The Compiler","text":"isqubo(model::MOI.ModelLike)\n\nTells if a given model is ready to be interpreted as a QUBO model.\n\nFor it to be true, a few conditions must be met:\n\nAll variables must be binary (MOI.VariableIndex ∈ MOI.ZeroOne)\nNo other constraints are allowed\nThe objective function must be of type MOI.ScalarQuadraticFunction, MOI.ScalarAffineFunction or MOI.VariableIndex\nThe objective sense must be either MOI.MIN_SENSE or MOI.MAX_SENSE\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.isqubo"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.copy!","page":"The Compiler","text":"copy!(model::Virtual.Model, arch::AbstractArchitecture)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.copy!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.build!","page":"The Compiler","text":"build!(model::Virtual.Model, arch::AbstractArchitecture)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.build!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/6-compiler/#ToQUBO.Compiler.quadratize!","page":"The Compiler","text":"quadratize!(model::Virtual.Model, arch::AbstractArchitecture)\n\nQuadratizes the objective function from a model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Compiler.quadratize!"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/1-start/#Manual","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Manual"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/1-start/#Quick-Start-Guide","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Quick Start Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/1-start/#Using-the-Exact-Sampler","page":"Getting Started","text":"using JuMP\nusing ToQUBO\nusing QUBODrivers\n\nmodel = Model(() -> ToQUBO.Optimizer(ExactSampler.Optimizer))\n\n@variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n\n@objective(model, Max, 1.0 * x[1] + 2.0 * x[2] + 3.0 * x[3])\n\n@constraint(model, 0.3 * x[1] + 0.5 * x[2] + 1.0 * x[3] <= 1.6)\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nsolution_summary(model)","title":"Using the Exact Sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/1-start/#Using-PySA-(Simulated-Annealing)","page":"Getting Started","text":"using JuMP\nusing ToQUBO\nusing PySA\n\nmodel = Model(() -> ToQUBO.Optimizer(PySA.Optimizer))\n\n@variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n\n@objective(model, Max, 1.0 * x[1] + 2.0 * x[2] + 3.0 * x[3])\n\n@constraint(model, 0.3 * x[1] + 0.5 * x[2] + 1.0 * x[3] <= 1.6)\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nsolution_summary(model)","title":"Using PySA (Simulated Annealing)"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/1-start/#Table-of-Contents","page":"Getting Started","text":"Pages = [\"2-model.md\", \"3-results.md\", \"4-settings.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Table of Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/2-qubo/#QUBO","page":"QUBO","text":"","title":"QUBO"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/2-qubo/#Definition","page":"QUBO","text":"Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization, as the name suggests, refers to the global minimization or maximization of a quadratic polynomial on binary variables. A common presentation, the quadratic matrix form, is written as\n\nbeginarrayrl\n   min_mathbfx  mathbfx Qmathbfx \n   textrmst      mathbfx in mathbbB^n\nendarray\n\nwhere Q in mathbbR^n times n is symmetric and mathbbB = lbrace0 1rbrace. Note that, since x^2 = x holds for x in mathbbB, the linear terms of the objective function are stored in the main diagonal of Q.","title":"Definition"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/2-qubo/#OK,-but-why-QUBO?","page":"QUBO","text":"Mathematically speaking, there is a notorious equivalence between QUBO and Max-Cut problems, e.g. for every QUBO instance there is an information preserving Max-Cut reformulation and vice versa. This statement is followed by two immediate implications:\n\nIn the general case, solving QUBO globally is NP-Hard.\nIt is a simple yet expressive mathematical programming framework.\n\nImplication 1. tells us that such problems are computationally intractable and that heuristics and metaheuristics are to be employed instead of exact methods. No 2. relates to the fact that we are able to represent many other optimization models by means of the QUBO formalism.\n\nThe Ising Model, on the other hand, is a mathematical abstraction to describe statistical interactions within mechanical systems with interesting properties for encoding combinatorial problems. Its Hamiltonian leads to an optimization formulation in terms of the spin values of their states, given by\n\nbeginarrayrl\n   min_mathbfs  mathbfhmathbfs + mathbfs Jmathbfs \n   textrmst      mathbfs in lbrace-1+1rbrace^n\nendarray\n\nwith strictly upper triangular J in mathbbR^n times n and mathbfh in mathbbR.\n\n(Image: D-Wave Washington 1000Q)\n\nThe Ising reformulation alternative draws the bridge between QUBO problems and devices designed to sample global or approximate ground states of the Ising Hamiltonian with high probability[Mohseni2022]. Some of the paradigms that stand out in this context are quantum gate-based optimization algorithms (QAOA and VQE), quantum annealers, hardware-accelerated platforms (Coherent Ising Machines and Simulated Bifurcation Machines) and physics-inspired methods (Simulated Annealing, Parallel Tempering). The significant advances in these computing systems contributed to the growing popularity of the model across the literature.\n\n[Mohseni2022]: Mohseni, N., McMahon, P. L. & Byrnes, T. Ising machines as hardware solvers of combinatorial optimization problems. Nat Rev Phys 4, 363–379 (2022). {arXiv}","title":"OK, but why QUBO?"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/8-appendix/#Appendix","page":"Appendix","text":"","title":"Appendix"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/8-appendix/#ToQUBO.jl's-Assets","page":"Appendix","text":"","title":"ToQUBO.jl's Assets"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/8-appendix/#Logo","page":"Appendix","text":"The ideia behind ToQUBO.jl's logo comes from a wordplay in Portuguese and Spanish. The package's main purpose is to assemble QUBO Models, which sounds like cubo[1], the translation for cube.\n\n(Image: ToQUBO.jl Logo)","title":"Logo"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/8-appendix/#Colors","page":"Appendix","text":"The colors were picked according to  Julia's Reference for logo graphics[2]. Text color matches the innermost shape and renders fairly well in both light and dark background themes.","title":"Colors"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/8-appendix/#Typography","page":"Appendix","text":"The MADETYPE Sunflower[3] font was chosen. It was converted to a SVG path using the Google Font to Svg Path[4] online tool.\n\n[1]: IPA: [ˈkubʊ]\n\n[2]: github.com/JuliaLang/julia-logo-graphics\n\n[3]: Licensed by the authors for use in this project\n\n[4]: danmarshall.github.io/google-font-to-svg-path","title":"Typography"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/8-appendix/#Web-Icon-![ToQUBO.jl-Icon](../assets/favicon.ico)","page":"Appendix","text":"The icon used to decorate the documentation resembles an assembled version of the cube with its blue face making up the background.","title":"Web Icon (Image: ToQUBO.jl Icon)"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/7-solvers/#QUBO-Solvers","page":"Solvers","text":"","title":"QUBO Solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/7-solvers/#Solvers,-Annealers-and-Samplers","page":"Solvers","text":"ToQUBO.jl's main goal is to make use of parameterized stochastic optimization solvers, particularly those relying on non-conventional hardware such as Quantum Annealing and other Ising Machines. A few MOI-compliant interfaces for annealers and samplers are bundled within ToQUBO.jl via the QUBODrivers.jl companion package. Some of them are presented below.","title":"Solvers, Annealers & Samplers"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/7-solvers/#Simulated-Annealing","page":"Solvers","text":"Provided by D-Wave's open-source code libraries, this Simulated Annealing engine implements some of the features and configurations you would find using the Quantum API. Its adoption is recommended for basic usage, tests, and research due to its robustness, simplicity and ease of use. The DWave.jl's DWave.Neal module uses QUBODrivers.jl to deliver an interface to this sampler.","title":"Simulated Annealing"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/7-solvers/#Quantum-Annealing","page":"Solvers","text":"Interfacing with D-Wave's quantum annealer is one of the milestones we expect to achieve with this package. Like other proprietary optimization resources such as Gurobi, FICO® Xpress and IBM® CPLEX®, this requires licensing and extra steps are needed to get an access token. In a first moment, for those willing to get started, the DWave.Neal optimizer might be enough to learn the ropes.","title":"Quantum Annealing"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/7-solvers/#Random-Sampling","page":"Solvers","text":"This sampler is implemented for test purposes and simply assigns 0 or 1 to each variable according to a given probability bias 0 le p le 1, which defaults to p = 05. After running the using QUBODrivers command, RandomSampler.Optimizer will be available.","title":"Random Sampling"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/7-solvers/#Exact-Solver-(Exhaustive-Enumeration)","page":"Solvers","text":"Also made to be used in tests, the ExactSolver.Optimizer interface runs through all possible state configurations, which implies in an exponential time complexity on the number of variables. Thus, only problems with at most approxeq 20 variables should be provided since visiting 2^20 approxeq 10^6 states can already take up to a few seconds.","title":"Exact Solver (Exhaustive Enumeration)"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/7-solvers/#Mixed-Integer-Quadratic-Programming","page":"Solvers","text":"The most accessible alternative to the forementioned methods are Mixed-Integer Quadratic Programming (MIQP) solvers such as Gurobi, CPLEX, SCIP and BARON. These are not intended to be of regular use alongside ToQUBO.jl since providing a QUBO reformulation will usually make things harder for non-specialized solvers. Yet, there are still a few cases where they may be suitable, such as tests, benchmarks, or any other situation where global optimality is a must.","title":"Mixed-Integer Quadratic Programming"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/","page":"Examples","text":"<!– Generated by docs/make.jl – do not edit directly. –>","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Examples-in-QUBO.jl","page":"Examples","text":"Application-oriented examples for the JuliaQUBO ecosystem now live in the QUBO.jl documentation, which serves as the canonical entry point for workflows that combine the compiler, drivers, and tooling packages.\n\nToQUBO.jl keeps the package-specific workflow and reference material in this manual. For a local walkthrough of building and solving a model with ToQUBO.Optimizer, see Running a Model.\n\nThese links point to the latest released QUBO.jl documentation.\n\nThe current example pages in QUBO.jl are:\n\nKnapsack, a small binary model with a capacity constraint that compiles to a compact QUBO.\nPrime Factorization, a bounded integer model that highlights automatic encoding and quadratization.\nPortfolio Optimization, a small continuous allocation model that shows how bounded encodings affect the compiled problem size.","title":"Examples in QUBO.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Running-a-Model","page":"Running a Model","text":"This section describes how to create, configure, and run optimization models using ToQUBO.jl.","title":"Running a Model"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Creating-a-Model","page":"Running a Model","text":"To use ToQUBO.jl, you need to create a JuMP model with the ToQUBO.Optimizer as the optimizer. The ToQUBO.Optimizer wraps an underlying QUBO solver that will actually solve the reformulated problem.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing ToQUBO\nusing QUBODrivers  # Provides QUBO solver interfaces\n\n# Create a model with a QUBO solver (e.g., ExactSampler for small problems)\nmodel = Model(() -> ToQUBO.Optimizer(ExactSampler.Optimizer))\n\nnote: Choosing a QUBO Solver\nThe inner optimizer must be a QUBO-compatible solver. Common choices include:ExactSampler.Optimizer from QUBODrivers.jl for exact solutions on small problems\nQuantum annealing backends like D-Wave\nSimulated annealing solversReplace the solver with your preferred QUBO backend.\n\nThe ToQUBO.Optimizer acts as a bridge between your JuMP model and QUBO solvers. It automatically translates your problem into QUBO form and passes it to the underlying solver.","title":"Creating a Model"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Defining-Variables","page":"Running a Model","text":"ToQUBO.jl supports various variable types that are automatically encoded into binary variables:","title":"Defining Variables"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Binary-Variables","page":"Running a Model","text":"Binary variables are natively supported since QUBO problems are defined over binary variables.\n\n@variable(model, x, Bin)\n@variable(model, y[1:n], Bin)","title":"Binary Variables"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Integer-Variables","page":"Running a Model","text":"Integer variables within a bounded interval are encoded using binary expansions.\n\n@variable(model, 0 <= z <= 10, Int)","title":"Integer Variables"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Continuous-Variables","page":"Running a Model","text":"Continuous variables within a bounded interval are discretized and encoded.\n\n@variable(model, 0.0 <= w <= 1.0)\n\ntip: Variable Bounds\nAll non-binary variables must have finite bounds for ToQUBO.jl to encode them properly.","title":"Continuous Variables"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Defining-Constraints","page":"Running a Model","text":"ToQUBO.jl supports linear and quadratic constraints, which are reformulated as penalty terms in the QUBO objective function. Equality constraints use a quadratic residual penalty by default. A signed linear residual can be selected with Attributes.LinearPenalty(), but it requires an explicit Attributes.ConstraintEncodingPenaltyHint() because the compiler cannot infer a safe coefficient for that form automatically. See Constraint Penalty Methods for the settings and an example.\n\nThe optimizer accepts scalar affine and scalar quadratic constraints with <=, >=, and == bounds. It also accepts SOS1 constraints and MOI indicator constraints whose inner scalar constraint is affine or quadratic and uses an EqualTo, LessThan, GreaterThan, or Interval bound set. Indicator constraints may activate on either zero or one.","title":"Defining Constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Linear-Constraints","page":"Running a Model","text":"# Less than or equal\n@constraint(model, 2*y[1] + 3*y[2] <= 5)\n\n# Equal to\n@constraint(model, y[1] + y[2] == 1)\n\n# Greater than or equal (automatically converted)\n@constraint(model, y[1] + y[2] >= 1)","title":"Linear Constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Quadratic-Constraints","page":"Running a Model","text":"# Less than or equal\n@constraint(model, y[1] * y[2] + y[3] <= 1)\n\n# Equal to\n@constraint(model, y[1] * y[2] + y[3] == 1)\n\n# Greater than or equal\n@constraint(model, y[1] * y[2] + y[3] >= 1)","title":"Quadratic Constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#SOS1-Constraints","page":"Running a Model","text":"Special ordered sets of type 1 are supported over VectorOfVariables.\n\n@variable(model, s[1:3], Bin)\n@constraint(model, s in SOS1())","title":"SOS1 Constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Indicator-Constraints","page":"Running a Model","text":"JuMP/MOI represents indicator constraints as a vector function in an Indicator set. ToQUBO compiles both VectorAffineFunction and VectorQuadraticFunction indicator forms when the inner scalar constraint uses <=, >=, ==, or an interval bound.\n\n@variable(model, a, Bin)\n\n# Affine inner constraint\n@constraint(model, a => {2*y[1] + y[2] <= 1})\n\n# Quadratic inner constraint\n@constraint(model, a => {y[1] * y[2] + y[3] <= 1})\n\n# Interval inner constraint\n@constraint(model, a => {0 <= y[1] * y[2] + y[3] <= 1})","title":"Indicator Constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Generalized-Disjunctive-Programming","page":"Running a Model","text":"Generalized disjunctive programming models can be written with DisjunctiveProgramming.jl and solved through ToQUBO when they are reformulated with Indicator(). In that workflow, DisjunctiveProgramming.jl turns logical disjuncts into JuMP/MOI indicator constraints. ToQUBO then compiles those affine or quadratic indicator constraints into the QUBO objective. Add DisjunctiveProgramming.jl to your project environment when using this workflow.\n\nToQUBO's maintained GDP support boundary is this indicator-constraint compilation path. DisjunctiveProgramming.jl remains the modeling and reformulation package, ToQUBO remains the optimizer backend, and ToQUBO does not expose a separate GDP-specific runtime API. The historical DisjunctiveToQUBO.jl artifact did not introduce production package code that needs to be migrated into ToQUBO; ongoing support is maintained here through indicator-constraint compilation, tests, and examples. The underlying paper is Xavier, Pedro Maciel, Pedro Ripper, Joshua Pulsipher, Joaquim Dias Garcia, Nelson Maculan, and David E. Bernal Neira. \"Disjunctive programming meets QUBO.\" In Computer Aided Chemical Engineering, vol. 53, pp. 3433-3438. Elsevier, 2024. ScienceDirect.\n\nThe compact example below is a reduced two-corner disjunction: x must lie either at the lower-left point or the upper-right point. It keeps the compiled QUBO small enough for ExactSampler.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing ToQUBO\nusing QUBODrivers\nusing DisjunctiveProgramming\n\nmodel = GDPModel(() -> ToQUBO.Optimizer(ExactSampler.Optimizer))\n\n@variable(model, -1 <= x[1:2] <= 1)\n@variable(model, Y[1:2], Logical)\n\n@constraint(model, [i = 1:2], x[i] == -1, Disjunct(Y[1]))\n@constraint(model, [i = 1:2], x[i] == 1, Disjunct(Y[2]))\n@disjunction(model, Y)\n\n@objective(model, Min, x[1] + x[2])\n\nset_attribute.(x, ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingMethod(), ToQUBO.Encoding.Unary())\nset_attribute.(x, ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingBits(), 1)\n\noptimize!(model; gdp_method = Indicator())\n\ntermination_status(model), primal_status(model), value.(x)\n\nThis integration does not require a separate DisjunctiveToQUBO.jl package. Use DisjunctiveProgramming.jl for GDP modeling and reformulation, then use ToQUBO.Optimizer as the optimizer backend.","title":"Generalized Disjunctive Programming"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Paper-Artifact-Repository","page":"Running a Model","text":"The historical pedromxavier/DisjunctiveToQUBO.jl repository is a paper/reproducibility artifact, not a maintained ToQUBO runtime package. Its notebooks, generated result files, PDFs, plots, and pinned notebook environments should stay outside ToQUBO's normal package tests and documentation CI. Cite the associated paper as Xavier et al. (2024), \"Disjunctive programming meets QUBO,\" Computer Aided Chemical Engineering, vol. 53, pp. 3433-3438.\n\nMaintained GDP usage examples belong in this manual. The artifact repository should either be archived as-is or keep its current ownership with a README notice that points readers to the maintained ToQUBO documentation for supported GDP workflows. Transferring it under JuliaQUBO should only be considered if the organization chooses to steward paper artifacts separately from production packages.","title":"Paper Artifact Repository"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Defining-the-Objective-Function","page":"Running a Model","text":"The objective function can be linear or quadratic. ToQUBO.jl supports both minimization and maximization.\n\n# Linear objective\n@objective(model, Max, 2*y[1] + 3*y[2] + 4*y[3])\n\n# Quadratic objective\n@objective(model, Min, y[1]*y[2] + y[2]*y[3] + y[1])","title":"Defining the Objective Function"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Running-the-Optimization","page":"Running a Model","text":"Once your model is defined, call optimize! to solve it:\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nThis function performs the following steps:\n\nCompilation: ToQUBO.jl translates your JuMP model into QUBO form\nSolving: The underlying QUBO solver finds solutions\nTranslation: Results are mapped back to your original variables","title":"Running the Optimization"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Checking-Solution-Status","page":"Running a Model","text":"After optimization, check the termination status and primal status to verify the solve succeeded:\n\n# Check if the solver found a solution\ntermination_status(model)\n\n# Check the status of the primal solution\nprimal_status(model)\n\n# Get a human-readable summary\nsolution_summary(model)","title":"Checking Solution Status"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Working-with-Multiple-Solutions","page":"Running a Model","text":"Many QUBO solvers return multiple solutions. You can iterate over them:\n\n# Get the number of solutions found\nnum_solutions = result_count(model)\n\n# Access specific solutions\nfor i in 1:num_solutions\n    xi = value.(x, result = i)\n    obj = objective_value(model, result = i)\n    println(\"Solution $i: x = $xi, objective = $obj\")\nend","title":"Working with Multiple Solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Example:-Complete-Workflow","page":"Running a Model","text":"Here's a complete example demonstrating the typical workflow:\n\nusing JuMP\nusing ToQUBO\nusing QUBODrivers  # Provides ExactSampler and other solvers\n\n# 1. Create model with a QUBO solver\nmodel = Model(() -> ToQUBO.Optimizer(ExactSampler.Optimizer))\n\n# 2. Define binary variables\n@variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n\n# 3. Define constraints\n@constraint(model, x[1] + x[2] + x[3] <= 2)\n\n# 4. Define objective\n@objective(model, Max, x[1] + 2*x[2] + 3*x[3])\n\n# 5. Solve\noptimize!(model)\n\n# 6. Check status and retrieve results\nif termination_status(model) == OPTIMAL\n    println(\"Optimal solution found!\")\n    println(\"x = \", value.(x))\n    println(\"Objective = \", objective_value(model))\nend","title":"Example: Complete Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-model/#Next-Steps","page":"Running a Model","text":"Learn about Gathering Results to extract solution data\nExplore Compiler Settings to fine-tune the reformulation process","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/5-virtual/#Virtual-Mapping","page":"Virtual Mapping","text":"During reformulation, ToQUBO holds two distinct models, namely the Source Model and the Target Model. The source model is a generic MOI model restricted to the supported constraints. The target one is on the QUBO form used during the solving process. Both lie within a Virtual Model, which provides the necessary API integration and keeps all variable and constraint mapping tied together.\n\nThis is done in a transparent fashion for both agents since the user will mostly interact with the presented model, and the solvers will only access the generated one.","title":"Virtual Mapping"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/5-virtual/#Virtual-Model","page":"Virtual Mapping","text":"","title":"Virtual Model"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/5-virtual/#Virtual-Variables","page":"Virtual Mapping","text":"Every virtual model stores a collection of virtual variables, intended to provide a link between those in the source and those to be created in the target model. Each virtual variable stores encoding information for later expansion and evaluation.","title":"Virtual Variables"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/5-virtual/#ToQUBO.Virtual.Model","page":"Virtual Mapping","text":"Model{T}(optimizer::Union{Nothing, Type{<:MOI.AbstractOptimizer}} = nothing) where {T}\n\nThis Virtual Model links the final QUBO formulation to the original one, allowing variable value retrieving and other features.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Virtual.Model"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/5-virtual/#ToQUBO.Virtual.source","page":"Virtual Mapping","text":"source\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Virtual.source"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/5-virtual/#ToQUBO.Virtual.target","page":"Virtual Mapping","text":"target\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Virtual.target"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/5-virtual/#ToQUBO.Virtual.Variable","page":"Virtual Mapping","text":"Variable{T}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Virtual.Variable"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/5-virtual/#ToQUBO.Virtual.encoding","page":"Virtual Mapping","text":"encoding\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Virtual.encoding"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/5-virtual/#ToQUBO.Virtual.expansion","page":"Virtual Mapping","text":"expansion\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Virtual.expansion"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/5-virtual/#ToQUBO.Virtual.penaltyfn","page":"Virtual Mapping","text":"penaltyfn\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ToQUBO.Virtual.penaltyfn"},{"category":"section","location":"#ToQUBO.jl-Documentation","page":"Home","text":"ToQUBO.jl is a Julia Package intended to automatically translate models written in JuMP, into the QUBO mathematical optimization framework.","title":"ToQUBO.jl Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"julia> import Pkg\n\njulia> Pkg.add(\"ToQUBO\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Example","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Using-the-Exact-Sampler","page":"Home","text":"using JuMP\nusing ToQUBO\nusing QUBODrivers\n\nmodel = Model(() -> ToQUBO.Optimizer(ExactSampler.Optimizer))\n\n@variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n@objective(model, Max, 1.0*x[1] + 2.0*x[2] + 3.0*x[3])\n@constraint(model, 0.3*x[1] + 0.5*x[2] + 1.0*x[3] <= 1.6)\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nsolution_summary(model)","title":"Using the Exact Sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"#Using-PySA-(Simulated-Annealing)","page":"Home","text":"using JuMP\nusing ToQUBO\nusing PySA\n\nmodel = Model(() -> ToQUBO.Optimizer(PySA.Optimizer))\n\n@variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n@objective(model, Max, 1.0*x[1] + 2.0*x[2] + 3.0*x[3])\n@constraint(model, 0.3*x[1] + 0.5*x[2] + 1.0*x[3] <= 1.6)\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nsolution_summary(model)\n\nApplication-oriented examples now live in the Examples in QUBO.jl section.","title":"Using PySA (Simulated Annealing)"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing-ToQUBO.jl","page":"Home","text":"If you use ToQUBO.jl in your work, we kindly ask you to include the following citation:\n\n@software{toqubo:2023,\n  author       = {Pedro Maciel Xavier and Pedro Ripper and Tiago Andrade and Joaquim Dias Garcia and David E. Bernal Neira},\n  title        = {{ToQUBO.jl}},\n  month        = {feb},\n  year         = {2023},\n  publisher    = {Zenodo},\n  version      = {v0.1.9},\n  doi          = {10.5281/zenodo.7644291},\n  url          = {https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7644291}\n}\n\nFor the broader QUBO.jl ecosystem paper, cite the current preprint:\n\n@misc{xavier2023qubojl,\n  author       = {Pedro Maciel Xavier and Pedro Ripper and Tiago Andrade and Joaquim Dias Garcia and Nelson Maculan and David E. Bernal Neira},\n  title        = {{QUBO.jl: A Julia Ecosystem for Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization}},\n  year         = {2023},\n  eprint       = {2307.02577},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  primaryClass = {math.OC},\n  doi          = {10.48550/arXiv.2307.02577},\n  url          = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.02577}\n}","title":"Citing ToQUBO.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/integer_factorization/","page":"Prime Factorization","text":"<!– Generated by docs/make.jl – do not edit directly. –>","title":"Prime Factorization"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/integer_factorization/#Prime-Factorization","page":"Prime Factorization","text":"This page is kept as a compatibility entry for readers following older ToQUBO.jl example links.\n\nThe canonical version now lives in the QUBO.jl documentation:\n\nPrime Factorization\n\nFor package-specific workflow guidance, see Running a Model. For the current ecosystem examples index, see Examples in QUBO.jl.","title":"Prime Factorization"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/knapsack/","page":"Knapsack","text":"<!– Generated by docs/make.jl – do not edit directly. –>","title":"Knapsack"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/knapsack/#Knapsack","page":"Knapsack","text":"This page is kept as a compatibility entry for readers following older ToQUBO.jl example links.\n\nThe canonical version now lives in the QUBO.jl documentation:\n\nKnapsack\n\nFor package-specific workflow guidance, see Running a Model. For the current ecosystem examples index, see Examples in QUBO.jl.","title":"Knapsack"}]
